1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:03,090
Nobody trained this factory robot
to throw.

2
00:00:03,090 --> 00:00:07,006
It learned from a $73 plastic claw
and a GoPro.

3
00:00:07,167 --> 00:00:12,947
Normally, teaching a robot arm means
bolting a $40,000 machine to one table.

4
00:00:12,947 --> 00:00:15,075
Move it to a real kitchen,
and it fails.

5
00:00:15,235 --> 00:00:20,235
So Stanford, Columbia & MIT engineers
ditched the robot during training,

6
00:00:20,235 --> 00:00:22,843
carrying this 3D-printed claw
anywhere.

7
00:00:23,003 --> 00:00:28,333
Beside its fisheye lens, two tiny mirrors
create stereo 3D depth,

8
00:00:28,333 --> 00:00:31,720
while the GoPro's gyroscope tracks
fast wrist snaps.

9
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:35,870
Mount that claw on any robot arm,
match millisecond lag,

10
00:00:35,870 --> 00:00:37,803
and skills transfer zero-shot.

11
00:00:37,963 --> 00:00:42,333
Two arms fold a sweater.
One hurls apples into bins.

12
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:44,886
Another turns on a tap
to scrub ketchup.

13
00:00:45,036 --> 00:00:47,960
When researchers squirt extra mustard
mid-wash...

14
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:53,170
Nobody coded a retry, yet it scrubs
until the plate shines,

15
00:00:53,170 --> 00:00:57,050
works in blackouts, and serves espresso
on a water fountain.

16
00:00:57,050 --> 00:00:57,727
Remember...
